# SCLS publish endpoint — copy to publish.conf and edit. publish.conf is
# git-ignored and must stay that way.
#
# This file names a key; it must never CONTAIN one. The upload private key lives
# in ~/.ssh and never enters this tree. Nothing here is secret, but the hostname
# and account are site infrastructure and SCLS is published under
# BSD-3-Clause-LBNL — so they are configuration, not source.

# Where drops are uploaded, as user@host. The account should be a dedicated
# upload service account, not a person's login.
PUBLISH_REMOTE="upload-user@publish-host.example.org"

# Private key for that account. Give each build host its own key so one can be
# revoked without touching the others; the ssh-keygen comment is a good place to
# record which host it belongs to.
PUBLISH_KEY="$HOME/.ssh/scls_upload"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server-side expectations, for reference — configured on the publishing host,
# not here:
#
#   - The key is force-commanded to a write-only rsync confined to the staging
#     directory, so it cannot read, list, delete, obtain a shell, or escape the
#     sandbox with '..'. Verify all five before trusting a new endpoint; a
#     write-only key that can still write outside its sandbox is not contained.
#   - The host key is pinned in ~/.ssh/known_hosts. Check the fingerprint you
#     accept on first connect against one the publishing host states out of band
#     — trust-on-first-use is exactly where an impostor would sit.
#   - Uploads land in a staging area only. Promotion, signing and publication
#     are manual on the publishing host and are never triggered from here.
